 ### PPC Colorization via Forward Projection
-For a point $X$ in 3D space with world coordinates $X_w$, forward projection to camera coordinates $X_c$ is performed using the 4x4 extrinsic matrix $d$ (containing translation $t$ and rotation $R$): $\tilde{X}_c = d\tilde{X}_w$, where $\tilde{X}$ denotes homogeneous coordinates [S7]. 2D pixel coordinates $x_c$ are obtained by normalizing homogeneous image coordinates $\tilde{x}_c$ by depth, where $\tilde{x}_c = K\tilde{X}_c$, using the known camera intrinsic matrix $K$ [S7]. The center of each occupied voxel is used as the starting position to reduce aliasing artifacts [S7]. Occlusion is identified by grouping points projecting to the same pixel $(u,v)$ into set $P_{uv}$. The point with the minimum $z$-value in camera coordinates is marked as non-occluded ($P^*_{uv} = \arg \min_{P_i \in P_{uv}} z_i$), and remaining points are marked as occluded [S7]. Occluded voxels are filled with the average color of all non-occluded views for the same point, creating an 'RGB main' triplet suitable for renderers without plenoptic color support [S7].
+For a point $X$ in 3D space with world coordinates $X_w$, forward projection to camera coordinates $X_c$ is performed using the 4x4 extrinsic matrix $d$ (containing translation $t$ and rotation $R$): $\tilde{X}_c = d\tilde{X}_w$, where $\tilde{X}$ denotes homogeneous coordinates [S9]. 2D pixel coordinates $x_c$ are obtained by normalizing homogeneous image coordinates $\tilde{x}_c$ by depth, where $\tilde{x}_c = K\tilde{X}_c$, using the known camera intrinsic matrix $K$ [S9]. The center of each occupied voxel is used as the starting position to reduce aliasing artifacts [S9]. Occlusion is identified by grouping points projecting to the same pixel $(u,v)$ into set $P_{uv}$. The point with the minimum $z$-value in camera coordinates is marked as non-occluded ($P^*_{uv} = \arg \min_{P_i \in P_{uv}} z_i$), and remaining points are marked as occluded [S9]. Occluded voxels are filled with the average color of all non-occluded views for the same point, creating an 'RGB main' triplet suitable for renderers without plenoptic color support [S9].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ for $N$ points, as each point undergoes a constant number of matrix multiplications and divisions.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the input points and output pixel locations/masks.
-- **Strengths:** Efficiently maps 3D geometry to 2D image space, enabling visibility checks and pixel-based feature retrieval.
-- **Limitations:** Does not handle occlusion resolution within the projection step itself (requires additional logic like depth sorting or z-buffering). Projected eigenvectors are generally not orthogonal in 2D image space, even if the original 3D tensor was symmetric with orthogonal eigenvectors [S4, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When rendering 3D points to 2D images, computing visibility masks, or retrieving 2D features for 3D points.
-- **When not to use:** When occlusion handling is required as part of the projection (use Depth Estimation from Point Cloud or PPC Colorization instead).
